course: dictionary
lesson: 11767
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. To find the right one, read the whole sentence and look at the clues around the word. «The thief was barred from the shop for a year.» Barred here means not allowed to go in. The clue is the thief and the shop: a rule stopped him.
explain: «The old gate was barred with iron, so nobody could get in.» Barred here means blocked with bars. Gate and iron are the clues. Always ask: which meaning fits this sentence? Then try it in the sentence to check.
explain: «The owl had brown and white barred feathers.» Here barred means having stripes. Feathers are the clue, so this is about colour and pattern, not a rule or a lock. Same word, different meanings, and the context tells you which one to use.
fact: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one is right.
fact: «Barred» can mean banned, as in «barred from the club».
fact: «Barred» can mean having bars across it, as in «a barred window».
fact: «Barred» can mean having stripes, as in «barred feathers» on an owl.
fact: «Barred» is also the past form of bar, as in «The guard barred the way», which means blocked.
exercise: «She was barred from the pool after breaking the rules.» What does barred mean here? | Covered in bars | Swimming very fast | *Not allowed to go in | Painted with stripes
exercise: «The guard barred the way, so we could not pass.» What does barred mean? | *Blocked the way | Painted blue | Lit up brightly | Gave us a prize
exercise: «The tiger's fur was barred with black stripes.» Which meaning fits? | *Having stripes | Stuck fast | Having bars across it | Banned from the zoo
test: «The prison window was barred, so the prisoner could not climb out.» What does barred mean? | Striped like a zebra | Banned from the prison | *Having bars across it | Open wide
test: «Zara was barred from the library for a week.» What does barred mean here? | Locked with a key | Allowed in twice a week | *Not allowed to go in | Painted in stripes
exam: «No holds barred» means what? | *With no rules | Striped and bright | Banned from the game | With bars across the window
